  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 335 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nCurrently, a mid/upper level cutoff low has developed and is \ncentered over eastern Kansas and Missouri. Ridging is building into \nthe Western US. At the surface, this is translating into light \nnorthwesterly winds, mostly clear conditions, and continued below \nnormal temperatures. Morning lows across the area range from the \nupper teens into the lower 20s. Temperatures will struggle to \nrebound significantly today as we remain under the influence of the \nlower thicknesses. Highs will reach only into the lower to middle \n40s with lows tonight falling into the 20s once again.\n\nBroad ridging will finally begin to build in over the Plains by \nFriday leading to increasing thickness. While we will remain just on \nthe eastern periphery of the ridge, we can expect warmer conditions \nthrough the end of the forecast period with slightly above normal \ntemperatures continuing this weekend and into early next week. By \nFriday afternoon highs will reach into the middle to upper 50s and \nhighs around 60 will be seen for Saturday. A short wave associated \nwith a broad upper trough over Canada will sweep across Kansas on \nSaturday. Model guidance has come into better agreement regarding \nprecipitation chances for Saturday night. Scattered showers look to \noverspread the area generally after 00Z, and exit the region to the \neast by 12Z. Due to the timing of this activity, though most of the \nprecip will fall as rain, temperature profiles indicate there could \nbe some snow that mixes in at times. No winter impacts are expected \nand rainfall totals will remain light. High temperatures in the 50s \nand dry conditions will then continue through the end of the \nforecast period with locations in southern Kansas approaching 60 \nmany afternoons.",
